  "query_preview": "through Friday for areas generally along and south of a\nline from Mendota, IL, through Kankakee, IL, and Rensselaer, \nIN. \n\nThe threat for additional showers and thunderstorms in our area\nthe remainder of the day overall look low (20-30%), and of low \naerial coverage, owing to nebulous large scale forcing near the \nmid-level ridge axis. If any storms are able to develop this \nafternoon, their favored placement will be near the outflow \nboundary from far east central IL into northern IN. Otherwise, \nwe are looking to have a dry but muggy evening. \n\nTonight,an upper-level short-wave trough will track from the \nnorthern Plains across the Upper Midwest, driving a complex of \nthunderstorms across the lower Great Lakes. While the main \ncomplex may stay northeast of our area, renewed convection is \nplausible Friday morning along its southwestern flank as a \nsouthwesterly low-level jet interacts with a composite outflow \nboundary. This setup could support storm training and a \nlocalized flash flood threat, though confidence remains low with\nthe highest potential north of our area. Regardless, there is a\n30-50% chance of scattered showers and thunderstorms across far\nnortheastern IL and northwest IN between 2 am and 10 am Friday.\n\nFollowing morning activity, capping should maintain dry \nconditions through Friday afternoon. We are monitoring for \nscattered thunderstorms developing across southern Wisconsin \nlate Friday afternoon or evening along a cold front, which would\nlikely shift southeast into our area Friday night. The SPC has \nincluded our area in a Level 1 of 5 severe threat due to the \npotential for locally strong wind gusts.\n\nSurface high pressure building into the Great Lakes this weekend\nwill end our hot and humid spell, at least for a few days.\nTemperatures will trend toward the upper 70s near the lake to\nthe low 80s inland by Sunday. A moderate to high swim risk is\npossible at Lake Michigan beaches late Saturday through Sunday\ndue to persistent onshore flow and choppy conditions.\n\nKJB",
